WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.082 --> 00:00:06.540
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Hello and welcome.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.230
We're here at the Security Zone
at Google Next with Prajakta,

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.080
who is one of Google's
leading experts in denial

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.070
of service attacks.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:14.685
Hi, Prajakta.

00:00:14.685 --> 00:00:15.810
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Hi, Cassie.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:16.977
And thank you for having me.

00:00:16.977 --> 00:00:18.560
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
When companies deploy

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.300
internet-facing services,
one of the biggest problems

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.870
they encounter is denial
of service attacks.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.070
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So our
customers have a tough job.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.310
They want to quickly and
responsively serve their end

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.080
users and, at the
same time, they

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.120
need to defend their
internet facing services

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.980
against malicious attacks.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.680
And denial of service attacks
are getting much larger,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.490
they're getting much
more sophisticated,

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.890
and security teams need
to be prepared for them.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.062
So let's look at an example.

00:00:43.062 --> 00:00:44.520
Let's say you're
the infrastructure

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.650
manager for an online
retailer and it's

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.770
Black Friday, the busiest
day of the year for you,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.540
and you've got an important job.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.910
You need to make sure that
the website and apps stay up

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:54.810
and running.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.090
So Black Friday has
started, and here, we

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.420
can see all of the successful
transactions represented

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.570
as boxes coming into
the application servers.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.730
Everything looks to be
running smoothly now,

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.319
but let's see what happens
once an attack is launched.

00:01:08.319 --> 00:01:09.360
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Awesome.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.246
Do your worst.

00:01:10.246 --> 00:01:12.120
SPEAKER: The first attack
I'm going to launch

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.710
is a high volume DDoS attack.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.022
I'm going to use this to try
and overwhelm the servers

00:01:16.022 --> 00:01:16.980
with malicious traffic.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.661
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: We can see that
the application instances are

00:01:21.661 --> 00:01:23.540
being overwhelmed by
the malicious traffic

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.760
from the attacker's servers.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.510
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: If
this amount of traffic

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.310
keeps hitting the
application instances,

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.259
they'll get overloaded,
and they'll go down.

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:31.550
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: That's correct.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.350
Shoppers on the
website are already

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.600
getting a slow load
time and the website

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.410
is going to start crashing.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.760
Now, as the
infrastructure manager,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.805
you do have some tools at
your disposal to manage this.

00:01:42.805 --> 00:01:46.665
What do you think would be the
best way to go handle this?

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:49.040
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: I'm going to
go with infrastructure DDoS

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.880
protection using Google
Global Load Balancer.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.155
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Great choice.

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:56.861
After you enable the Google
Global Load Balancer,

00:01:56.861 --> 00:01:59.500
the application instances
are back up and running

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.250
and shoppers are
now able to access

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.200
the site without a problem.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.990
Now, in this example, you
turned on this defense

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.710
in the middle of a live attack.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.530
But in reality, you would
have actually already set up

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.860
your services behind the
Global Load Balancer,

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.310
prior to Black Friday.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.390
But Black Friday isn't over yet.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.659
In fact, our shoppers
are just getting started.

00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:22.700
Let's see what happens
when the attacker launches

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.940
an application attack this time.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.870
SPEAKER: This time, I'm going to
launch a SQL injection attack.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.328
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
now our attacker

00:02:29.328 --> 00:02:31.520
is sending in SQL injection
packets in an attempt

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.250
to bring down our
service and steal data.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.650
With SQL injection
one little flaw

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.690
in one little feature
of your application

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.850
can compromise all
the data for your app.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.070
Again, you've got some
tools at your disposal.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.260
What do you think is a
good defense for this type

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.760
of attack?

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.030
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: I'd pick Cloud
Armor SQL Injection Defense.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.530
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
Good choice, again.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.570
This Cloud Armor rule
acts as a defense

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.690
to these types of attacks
by automatically identifying

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.020
malicious SQL injection packets
and blocking them before they

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.750
hit your application instances.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:04.220
Without Cloud Armor
you would have

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.440
to first detect SQL
injection attack,

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.830
and then react, remediate, and
also fix your code and data.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:10.830
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Got it.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.400
So this means just
enabling this single rule

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.429
would defend our app against
all SQL injection attacks.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.720
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: That's correct.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.050
But the day isn't done yet,
and our sophisticated attacker

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.724
is going to come at us again.

00:03:22.724 --> 00:03:24.140
SPEAKER: For this
last attack, I'm

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.090
going to use a
multi-vector attack, which

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.220
is going to hit the
application instances using

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.680
multiple different types of
attack vectors all at once.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.891
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:32.999 --> 00:03:35.540
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Now, we can see
that a multi-vector attack is

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.840
hitting the servers and
trying to take them out.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.530
And it looks like
all of this traffic

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.270
is coming from one region.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.130
Again, the service
is slowing down

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.040
and our shoppers aren't
able to submit their orders.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.410
After analyzing this
attack, we find out

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.590
that it's coming from a single
location with a specific type

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.542
of cookie and user agent.

00:03:53.542 --> 00:03:54.530
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Aha.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.285
So maybe we could
create a custom rule

00:03:56.285 --> 00:03:58.730
in Cloud Armor that looks
for this cookie and user

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.070
agent in the traffic
coming from the region?

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:02.070
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Exactly.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:03.980
That's correct.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:05.850
Now you see the Cloud
Armor custom rule

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.730
blocking the
traffic that matches

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.640
our specified pattern, which
helps keep our service up

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.744
and running.

00:04:11.744 --> 00:04:13.160
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
I feel like now I

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.800
know a lot more about what
our retail customers have

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.821
to deal with on Black Friday.

00:04:17.821 --> 00:04:19.279
I have a lot more
empathy for them.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:20.992
It's pretty challenging.

00:04:20.992 --> 00:04:22.700
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: With
Google Cloud's tools

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.980
to help defend and scale
against this infrastructure

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.860
and application denial of
service attacks in place,

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.140
you can actually focus on
building great software

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.694
and great customer experiences.

00:04:31.694 --> 00:04:33.610
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Thanks
so much for walking us

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.230
through this demo, Prajakta.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.570
And thank you, [? Srina, ?]
for this very admirable attack

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:39.580
attempt.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.580
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.580
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Thank you
for having me, Cassie.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

